  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ahead of the 2024 Indonesian general elections, Australia also provided support for a CSO coalition in promoting women&#x2019;s representation in parliament. Through campaigns, policy discussions, and lobbying, the efforts had led to the issuance of the Supreme Court&#x2019;s ruling that ordering the General Election Commission&#x2019;s (KPU) and political parties to comply with the 30% women threshold mandated in the Election Law. This court ruling should enable hundreds of more potential women to run for 2029 legislative elections, promoting more inclusive procedural democracy in Indonesia.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported organisations for people with disabilities (OPDs) to conduct training, advocacy, and campaigns to promote more inclusive Indonesian general elections in February 2024. This support resulted in strengthened collaboration between OPDs and election management bodies (national and in 4 provinces) to deliver more accessible elections. Some regional election bodies adopted OPD partners&#x2019; recommendations by publishing data on voters with disabilities and updating the voter list. These efforts helped ensure eligible citizens with disabilities could exercise their democratic rights and improved the public&#x2019;s and election bodies&#x2019; awareness of the different needs of voters with disabilities. Support for OPDs increased the agency and influence of some people with disabilities as they increasingly took on roles in election committees. OPDs successfully ran a podcast on accessible elections that reached around 2,700 first-time voters and individuals with disabilities, safeguarding inclusive democracy.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through Demres partner, the Foundation for Islamic and Social Studies (YLKIS), Australia facilitated a coalition of civil society groups and government institutions to strengthen inclusive and accountable governance in Yogyakarta City. This coalition included 63 community-based organizations working across disability rights, gender equality, youth, environmental, and informal worker issues. They co-produced the Catatan Aspirasi Warga (Citizen Aspiration Notes), a citizen-led policy document presented at a multi- stakeholder forum in November 2024. For the first time, the city government formally adopted this document as a reference for the 2025&#x2013;2029 Regional Medium-Term Development Plan. YLKIS engaged five local agencies: the Regional Development Planning Agency, the Mayor&#x2019;s Office, the Regional General Election Commission, the Regional Election Supervisory Body, and the Trade Office, ensuring sustained dialogue between civil society and government. To extend citizen reach, YLKIS partnered with six private sector and media actors, including market vendors and digital platforms. Public accountability was further supported through Pemimpin Mendengar (Leaders Listening Initiative), a dialogue between the new mayor and community members held in May 2025. One concrete result was free toilet access for 200 women porters in local markets. It illustrates how coordinated civic engagement can lead to tangible policy and service reforms.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
